// Each chapter uses the same fictional neighborhood grocery service. Its numbers
// are illustrative, so the learner can see a decision develop across weeks.
export const chapters = [
  [
    {first:"A product is a repeatable way to help a particular group make progress. Begin with a situation, a job, and a result. A grocery app is only an interface: the product also includes catalog accuracy, picking, delivery, support, and recovery when an item is missing.",second:"A project has an end date and an agreed output; a product continues to earn use and improve outcomes. A service may be performed person to person. Many products combine software and service. The PM must understand the whole value path, because a beautiful screen cannot rescue unreliable fulfilment.",steps:["Write: ‘When [situation], [person] wants to [progress], so they can [outcome].’","Describe the current workaround and the cost of failure.","List every touchpoint needed to deliver the outcome, including operations and support."],case:"A shopper leaving work wants tonight’s ingredients delivered before cooking. The app promises a slot, but the store substitutes a key item without consent. The screen worked; the product outcome failed. First measure orders completed with acceptable substitutions and on-time arrival, then diagnose the broken step.",trap:"Calling every feature a product hides whether the customer achieved anything."},
    {first:"Value has two sides. Value creation means the customer’s job becomes easier, faster, safer, or more successful. Value capture means the provider earns enough to keep delivering that result. Both sides must hold over repeated use.",second:"In B2C the individual often uses and pays. In B2B, an employee may use the tool, a manager may buy it, and security may approve it. In B2G, procurement and public accountability add constraints. Map these roles before treating one person’s enthusiasm as a purchase decision.",steps:["Name the user, buyer, approver, and any supply-side participant.","Write the benefit each receives and the cost or risk each carries.","Estimate the unit: price per order minus variable fulfilment and support cost."],case:"The grocery service charges ₹35 delivery on a ₹400 basket. If picking and delivery cost ₹58, each order loses ₹23 before fixed costs. Raising fees may protect margin but reduce adoption. The PM tests whether a larger basket, better routing, or a narrower service area improves both shopper success and contribution margin.",trap:"Revenue alone does not prove value; usage alone does not prove a sustainable business."},
    {first:"A PM does not own every decision or act as a ticket dispatcher. The role is to bring customer, business, design, engineering, and operational evidence into choices the team can explain. The product owner role may focus more on a delivery backlog; the exact division varies by organization.",second:"A useful PM cycle is: understand the outcome, discover the problem, compare options, decide a test, deliver safely, and learn from results. Product sense is the habit of noticing the job beneath a request. AI can speed synthesis and drafts, but its fluent output still needs evidence and verification.",steps:["Translate a feature request into user, situation, desired outcome, and evidence question.","Ask design and engineering which experience and technical constraints matter.","Record the decision, owner, measure, and condition that would change it."],case:"A stakeholder asks for an AI shopping assistant. The PM investigates what shoppers struggle with: finding substitutions, planning a basket, or tracking delivery. If missing-item anxiety dominates, a consent-based substitute flow may solve the job with less cost and error risk than a chatbot.",trap:"Being the loudest decision maker is not the same as making a decision well."}
  ],
  [
    {first:"Strategy is a set of choices about where to play and how to win. Vision names a future worth pursuing; mission names the organization’s ongoing purpose; strategy chooses a customer, problem, advantage, and exclusions. A roadmap sequences bets that express those choices.",second:"A strategy becomes useful when two attractive requests conflict. A focused segment gives the team a chance to learn deeply and build something distinctive. A claim such as ‘for everyone’ gives little guidance on pricing, design, distribution, or what to postpone.",steps:["Choose one segment by job and constraints, not only age or geography.","State its current alternative, important unmet need, and credible advantage.","Write two tempting segments or features you will defer, and why."],case:"The grocery service chooses apartment residents within two kilometers of three partner stores who need dependable dinner essentials. It delays citywide expansion and restaurant meals. The limited radius makes fulfilment more predictable and gives a clear test of repeat weekly orders.",trap:"A roadmap full of features is an output plan, not a strategy."},
    {first:"Positioning explains why a particular customer should choose this product over the real alternatives. Alternatives include a competitor, a manual workaround, and doing nothing. Differentiation matters only if customers value it and can believe the promise.",second:"A moat is an advantage that becomes hard to copy over time. It could be distribution, workflow integration, trusted supply, data rights, or scale economics. A large feature list is rarely a moat. In B2B, purchase, migration, training, permissions, and procurement are part of the competitive choice.",steps:["Describe the moment of choice and the alternatives available then.","Compare alternatives on the outcome, total effort, trust, and price.","Write one testable position: ‘For [segment], we deliver [outcome] because [credible proof].’"],case:"The grocery service competes with a neighborhood shop, a large marketplace, and the shopper’s habit of stopping on the way home. It positions around accurate essentials in a reliable evening slot. A test compares promise comprehension and repeat purchase, not only clicks on the homepage.",trap:"A feature comparison can miss the customer’s switching cost or their preference for an existing habit."},
    {first:"A point solution solves one bounded workflow. A platform offers shared capabilities to several workflows or teams. Start with a narrow wedge when evidence, delivery capacity, and repeat demand are uncertain. Generalize only what real use cases share.",second:"Land and expand means winning a first use case, proving value, then broadening adoption or spend. Retention is a prerequisite: expansion built on a weak first experience multiplies support burden. Pricing and go-to-market must fit who receives value and who can authorize payment.",steps:["Define the first workflow and the proof of repeated value.","List capabilities that might be reused, but mark them as hypotheses.","Set a threshold for expansion: adoption, quality, unit economics, and operational readiness."],case:"After reliable dinner baskets in three neighborhoods, the grocery service considers office pantry restocking. Shared catalog and routing may help, but purchasing roles and delivery windows differ. The team pilots one office before turning consumer checkout into a general procurement platform.",trap:"Building platform infrastructure before a validated first job can make the product broad and weak."}
  ],
  [
    {first:"Discovery reduces uncertainty before a large commitment. Start with the decision: are you trying to understand a problem, estimate how often it occurs, compare solutions, or test usability? Each question needs different evidence.",second:"Interviews uncover context and language; observation reveals behavior and workarounds; surveys estimate reported patterns in a defined sample; analytics show recorded behavior; experiments compare outcomes under controlled changes. Secondary research helps map a market, but it rarely proves a local user problem.",steps:["Write the decision that research could change.","Choose a method and recruitment group that can answer it.","Record limitations: sample, timing, self-report, and missing voices."],case:"The grocery team hears that substitutions are frustrating. It observes ten recent order resolutions, interviews shoppers and pickers about specific incidents, then surveys a representative recent-order sample about frequency. The methods answer different questions: how failure happens and how widespread it may be.",trap:"‘Would you use this feature?’ often measures politeness or imagination, not behavior."},
    {first:"Evidence quality is about fit to the claim. A direct observation can support ‘this happened in the observed task.’ It cannot by itself support ‘most shoppers experience this.’ A population claim needs sampling and a clear denominator. Keep observation, interpretation, and decision separate.",second:"Market sizing has the same discipline. TAM is total demand under a defined market; SAM is the serviceable part; SOM is a plausible obtainable share. State unit, geography, time period, price, and adoption assumptions. CAGR describes historical or projected growth of a defined market, not guaranteed product growth.",steps:["Label each claim as observed fact, estimate, hypothesis, or unknown.","For a number, record source, sample or denominator, period, and definition.","Ask what counterevidence would change the decision."],case:"Suppose 100,000 nearby households buy groceries, but only 18,000 are in delivery range and 5,000 have the target evening job. These are illustrative filters, not a forecast. A pilot must still test reachable acquisition, order frequency, and margin before the team predicts share.",trap:"A large market headline does not establish demand for one feature or a reachable business."},
    {first:"A persona is a decision tool, not an invented character. Segment people when a difference in job, context, behavior, authority, or constraint changes the product choice. In B2B, distinguish the daily user, administrator, buyer, and approver.",second:"Competitive analysis also follows the job. Compare direct products, adjacent options, and manual work. Five Forces can reveal supplier, buyer, substitute, entrant, and competitive pressure; it is a market lens, not a substitute for interviewing users. Watch discoverability, actual use, and retention separately.",steps:["Create a segment from observed differences in behavior or constraints.","Write what each segment is trying to accomplish and what blocks it.","Show one product or distribution decision that changes because of the segment."],case:"Busy evening shoppers care about slot reliability; planned weekly shoppers may value assortment and price. The team should not design one default journey for both merely because both live nearby. It tests whether the first segment’s repeat use is strong enough before widening the offer.",trap:"Age, hobby, and stock-photo details are noise if they do not change a decision."}
  ],
  [
    {first:"A problem statement defines who struggles, in what situation, what progress they seek, and the consequence. Jobs to Be Done describes that desired progress rather than a feature. A good frame is narrow enough to investigate and broad enough to allow multiple solutions.",second:"Use research signals to form a hypothesis, then test alternatives. ‘How might we’ is a prompt for exploring options after the problem is bounded. The Double Diamond offers two cycles: broaden and narrow the understanding of the problem, then broaden and narrow possible solutions.",steps:["Describe person, situation, desired progress, observed friction, and consequence.","Remove any named feature or technology from the problem sentence.","List evidence for the statement and the part still uncertain."],case:"For evening shoppers ordering essentials, unexpected substitutions after checkout make dinner planning unreliable. The team has observed complaints and cancellation events but does not yet know which item categories or consent moments drive the most harm. That is a researchable problem, unlike ‘build an AI substitute bot.’",trap:"A solution hidden inside the problem statement closes exploration too early."},
    {first:"A metric drop tells you where to investigate, not why it happened. Decompose the journey, verify instrumentation, segment affected users, and collect qualitative evidence. Five Whys can generate possible causal chains, but there may be several interacting causes.",second:"System effects matter. A fee change might lower checkout conversion while improving contribution margin; a faster fulfilment promise might raise conversion while increasing late deliveries. Diagnose both the customer effect and the operational constraint before selecting a solution.",steps:["Confirm event definitions and the size of the change.","Segment by device, cohort, category, geography, and failure state.","Compare competing explanations with logs, observation, and recent-event interviews."],case:"Checkout conversion falls from 60% to 50% in one week. The drop is concentrated among orders with fresh produce after a new substitution policy. The team checks fee changes and payment errors too, then interviews affected shoppers. Only after that does it choose between clearer consent, better inventory accuracy, or a different policy.",trap:"The first plausible story is not proof of root cause."},
    {first:"Prioritization chooses a sequence under limited time, people, and risk tolerance. RICE estimates reach × impact × confidence ÷ effort for comparable ideas. MoSCoW helps separate must, should, could, and won’t for a particular scope; Kano asks how a capability affects satisfaction; impact–effort is a quick discussion aid.",second:"None of these scores outranks a legal requirement, safety control, hard dependency, or strategic choice. A user story records a role, goal, and benefit; acceptance criteria describe observable behavior. The backlog holds delivery work; the roadmap explains outcome bets and their timing or confidence.",steps:["State the outcome and shortlist comparable options.","Show estimates, sources, confidence, constraints, and dependencies.","Choose one option, one deferred option, and a signal that would change the choice."],case:"A colorful discovery carousel scores high on reach, but an inventory synchronization fix is needed to prevent false availability. The team ships the data fix first because it protects the core promise, then tests a small consent-flow change. The roadmap shows reliability before discovery growth.",trap:"A precise spreadsheet can disguise weak assumptions and mandatory work."}
  ],
  [
    {first:"UX is the full experience of accomplishing a job; UI is the visible interface within it. Utility asks whether the product solves the job. Usability asks whether people can accomplish it without needless effort. Visual design helps hierarchy, meaning, and trust, but cannot replace either.",second:"Map the journey from entry through action, feedback, error, and recovery. Information architecture decides where information lives. Interaction design decides what happens after an action. UX writing makes choices and consequences understandable. Cognitive load rises when people must remember hidden rules or decode unfamiliar labels.",steps:["Pick one user goal and map every touchpoint, including outside the app.","Mark uncertainty, waiting, error, and recovery moments.","Decide which friction blocks the goal and which is only cosmetic."],case:"A grocery checkout may look polished but say ‘processing’ after payment with no order status. Shoppers retry, creating duplicate charges. The high-value UX fix is a clear transaction state and safe recovery path, not another visual refresh.",trap:"Judging a journey from one screenshot misses behavior and failure states."},
    {first:"A prototype is a cheap representation used to answer a question before full build. A paper sketch can test navigation labels; a clickable flow can test task sequence; a functional slice may be needed to test speed or data quality. Fidelity should match the uncertainty.",second:"PM, design, and engineering collaborate from discovery onward. Design can expose comprehension problems; engineering can expose constraints and cheaper alternatives; operations can expose service failures. Usability testing asks a participant to attempt a real task while the team observes where understanding breaks.",steps:["Write the assumption and task the prototype will test.","Build the smallest representation that preserves the critical decision.","Observe several task attempts; record behavior before interpreting it."],case:"The grocery team sketches two substitution-consent flows. A shopper must approve a substitute before a promised cutoff. Testing reveals that ‘similar item’ is ambiguous; shoppers need price and dietary differences. The prototype changes before any inventory integration is built.",trap:"A polished clickable happy path does not test empty, loading, error, or edge states."},
    {first:"Heuristics are rules of thumb for inspecting a design. Visibility of system status, familiar language, consistency, user control, and error recovery are useful lenses. Accessibility asks whether people can perceive, operate, and understand the experience across abilities and devices.",second:"A heuristic finding is a hypothesis about impact, not measured harm. Pair it with task observation and severity. A design system provides reusable patterns, but each use still needs context, labels, focus order, contrast, touch size, and clear recovery.",steps:["Observe a specific task and note exactly where someone hesitates or fails.","Name the relevant principle and likely consequence.","Propose the smallest change and retest the same task, including keyboard and narrow screens."],case:"The substitution approval appears as a tiny icon that disappears after scrolling. The team records missed approvals, then tests a persistent labeled action with keyboard access. Success is measured by understood decisions and fewer unwanted substitutions, not visual preference alone.",trap:"A UX law by itself does not prove that users have a problem."}
  ],
  [
    {first:"Communication is part of product work because decisions cross roles. Begin with the listener’s decision or action: approve a trade-off, implement a bounded behavior, prepare support, or understand a risk. Keep facts consistent while changing level of detail.",second:"A useful message states the outcome, current evidence, options or trade-off, recommendation, owner, and next step. Leadership may need impact and decision timing; engineering needs constraints and edge cases; support needs customer-visible behavior and recovery. Choose the channel that lets the audience act.",steps:["Write one sentence describing the action you need from the audience.","Lead with current status and evidence, then the decision and trade-off.","Record the agreed decision where affected teams can find it."],case:"The grocery pilot shows reliable orders in two neighborhoods but delays in the third. Leadership receives a decision to pause expansion; operations receives a staffing and routing diagnosis; support receives the affected order window and remedy. The numbers and uncertainty remain the same across all three messages.",trap:"An activity list without a decision or next action creates noise, not alignment."},
    {first:"A PRD is a shared record of a bounded product change: user problem, target, outcome, scope, requirements, constraints, measures, risks, and unresolved decisions. It does not replace conversation. A roadmap communicates direction; a prototype lets people inspect an experience; a ticket specifies work small enough to deliver.",second:"Write to the depth of the decision. A risky payment or authorization change needs failure handling and review; a copy change may need only a short brief. When a requirement changes, record the reason, affected teams, delivery effect, and who decided. Treat documents as living agreements, not ceremony.",steps:["Open with the user job and evidence, then desired measurable outcome.","Define in-scope behavior, exclusions, edge cases, and guardrails.","Review with design, engineering, operations, and any affected approver; log changes."],case:"The substitution PRD states that shoppers must see the replacement item, price difference, and cutoff time. It includes no-response behavior, picker fallback, refund logic, and success metrics. A prototype shows the interaction, while tickets divide the work. When the cutoff changes, the decision record captures the operational reason.",trap:"‘It is in the PRD’ does not resolve an ambiguity that readers interpret differently."},
    {first:"Launch communication tells people who gets a change, when, what they will see, how success and harm are watched, and how to recover. A progress update separates done, next, blocked, and decisions needed. An incident update starts with confirmed impact and current mitigation.",second:"Under pressure, distinguish known facts from suspected causes. Name an owner and the next update time. Root cause belongs in a later account when evidence supports it. For career or stakeholder storytelling, STAR can organize situation, task, action, and result, but never invent a metric or claim sole ownership of team work.",steps:["For launch: state audience, rollout size, support path, guardrails, and rollback trigger.","For incident: state confirmed impact, mitigation, owner, and next update.","After resolution: document cause, learning, and preventive action with evidence."],case:"A payment failure affects 8% of grocery checkouts on one method. The first update names the affected path, disables that method if safe, gives another payment route, and sets a 30-minute update cadence. The team does not announce a root cause until logs confirm it.",trap:"‘Fixed soon’ is reassurance without an impact boundary, owner, or recovery plan."}
  ],
  [
    {first:"A metric is a defined calculation, not a label on a chart. Start from the customer job or business decision, then specify event, unit, population, denominator, time window, and exclusions. ‘Active users’ can mean app opens or completed value actions; those tell different stories.",second:"Events record actions, properties add context, identity determines which actions belong to the same person or account, and cohorts group people by a shared starting condition. Bad instrumentation can create a false product conclusion. Validate a metric definition with raw examples before debating a trend.",steps:["Name the value action and eligible population.","Write numerator, denominator, event order, identity, and conversion window.","Test several real records and edge cases against the definition."],case:"For the grocery service, weekly successful dinner orders from first-month shoppers is more informative than app opens. An order counts only when delivered within the promised slot without an unresolved missing-item issue. A separate report tracks refunds and late deliveries.",trap:"‘Conversion increased’ is uninterpretable without the starting group and time window."},
    {first:"A metric ladder connects customer success to product outcomes, possible input drivers, and guardrails. A North Star candidate should reflect meaningful value delivered repeatedly. Input metrics are levers the team can influence; lagging outcomes show later results. These labels describe a hypothesis about a relationship, not proof of causation.",second:"OKRs separate objective (desired direction), key result (measured outcome), and initiative (proposed work). AARRR can organize acquisition, activation, retention, referral, and revenue; HEART can prompt happiness, engagement, adoption, retention, and task success. Use each framework to expose a question, not to fill a dashboard with every possible number.",steps:["Choose one value outcome and two or three plausible drivers.","Add quality, cost, trust, or safety guardrails.","State what experiment or analysis would test the proposed driver–outcome link."],case:"The grocery team’s candidate North Star is weekly reliable dinner orders. Possible drivers are accurate stock, slot availability, and substitution consent. Guardrails include late deliveries, refunds, picker workload, and order contribution margin. More orders do not count as success if reliability collapses.",trap:"A rising leading metric does not guarantee the customer outcome will rise."},
    {first:"A funnel measures progression through ordered steps; it identifies where eligible users stop advancing. A cohort follows a group that started under the same condition, such as first order in a given week. Retention asks whether that group returns for a meaningful action in a period that matches natural use.",second:"A funnel drop is a location, not an explanation. Check missing events, identity, eligibility, event order, and conversion windows before treating the chart as behavior. Then segment and combine with observation or interviews. Flow reports can expose unexpected paths; revenue and unit economics explain whether value can be sustained.",steps:["Define the ordered steps and whether rates are step-to-step or from the start.","Inspect drop-offs by cohort, device, geography, category, and failure event.","Form competing hypotheses and test them before recommending a fix."],case:"Of 100 illustrative shoppers who view an item, 54 add to cart, 31 begin checkout, and 19 pay. Overall view-to-pay conversion is 19%; checkout-to-pay is 19/31, about 61%. The funnel cannot tell whether payment errors, fees, delivery slots, or low intent explain the gap. A first-order cohort then shows whether paid shoppers reorder.",trap:"Do not call every non-converting visitor ‘lost’: some never intended to buy."}
  ]
];

export const glossary = [
  ["Product","A repeatable way to deliver a desired outcome to a defined customer group."],
  ["Product manager","The role that helps a team choose a customer problem, desired outcome, trade-offs, and evidence for learning."],
  ["Product management","The ongoing work of understanding a market and users, choosing a direction, delivering value, and learning from results."],
  ["Customer job","The progress someone wants to make in a particular situation."],
  ["Value proposition","The outcome promised to a chosen customer and why it is credible."],
  ["B2B / B2C / B2G","Selling to businesses, consumers, or government; user and buyer roles can differ."],
  ["Discovery","Work that reduces uncertainty about the problem, value, and solution."],
  ["Delivery","Work that builds, releases, and operates a usable solution."],
  ["Segment","A group whose shared job or constraints change a product decision."],
  ["Positioning","Why a target customer should choose this option over their alternatives."],
  ["TAM / SAM / SOM","Total demand, serviceable demand, and plausibly obtainable share under stated assumptions."],
  ["Persona","A research-grounded role or behavior pattern used to guide decisions."],
  ["JTBD","Jobs to Be Done: the progress sought in a particular circumstance."],
  ["RICE","A comparison score: reach times impact times confidence, divided by effort."],
  ["PRD","Product requirements document: a shared record of a bounded change and its rationale."],
  ["Prototype","A representation built to test a specific product or design assumption."],
  ["Usability","How effectively and easily people can complete a task."],
  ["North Star metric","A candidate measure of meaningful customer value delivered repeatedly."],
  ["Guardrail","A measure that should not deteriorate while improving a target outcome."],
  ["Funnel","An ordered sequence of events used to locate progression and drop-off."],
  ["Cohort","People or accounts grouped by a shared starting condition and time."],
  ["Retention","The share of a cohort returning to a meaningful action after a defined period."],
  ["OKR","An objective names a direction; key results measure outcomes."],
  ["Unit economics","Revenue and variable cost attributable to a unit such as an order or customer."],
  ["AI evaluation","A repeatable check that tests an AI system against task, quality, and risk criteria."]
];

// Short companion lessons retain ideas that sit between the three main
// chapters in each week's two sessions. They appear at the end of that week.
export const companionLessons = {
  1:[
    ["Need, want, desire","A need is the underlying progress a person seeks; a want is a preferred way to get it; a desire adds aspiration or emotion. ‘I need dinner ingredients’ can become ‘I want delivery before seven’ and ‘I want to feel in control of the evening.’ Research all three, but do not mistake a requested interface for the underlying need."],
    ["Product sense and signals","Product sense is a practiced ability to notice a meaningful job, a costly friction, and a plausible change. Use both leading signals, such as fewer substitution disputes, and lagging signals, such as reliable repeat orders. A leading signal is useful only when later outcomes confirm its relationship."],
    ["Different markets, different roles","In consumer products the shopper may choose and pay. In business products the user, buyer, administrator, and approver can be different people. In public-sector products, procurement, accessibility, and accountability may shape delivery. Map the roles before deciding whose feedback proves demand."]
  ],
  2:[
    ["Go-to-market and pricing","Go-to-market is how a chosen segment discovers, evaluates, buys, adopts, and receives support for the product. Pricing is a product choice: it affects who tries the service, what behavior it rewards, and whether delivery is sustainable. Test willingness and unit cost together; a low headline price can conceal a costly service model."],
    ["Retention before expansion","Retention shows that a first segment returns for meaningful value. Before entering another segment, inspect what drives repeat use, whether new users succeed, and what it costs to serve them. A land-and-expand plan is credible only when the first use case works without heroic manual effort."],
    ["PM and product owner","A PM commonly frames customer and business choices across discovery and delivery. A product owner commonly manages backlog clarity and sequencing in a delivery process. Organizations assign these duties differently. Judge the role by the decisions and accountability it carries, not its title."],
    ["Advantage versus feature parity","A differentiator helps a segment choose you now; a defensible advantage is harder to copy over time. Workflow fit, trusted supply, integrations, and lower switching effort may matter more than a longer feature list. Ask what a competitor could copy in a month and what takes repeated learning or operational capability."]
  ],
  3:[
    ["Primary and secondary research","Primary research collects new evidence for your decision, such as observing a grocery order. Secondary research interprets existing reports, market data, or competitor material. Use secondary sources to form questions and define context; validate local behavior with methods that reach the people and situations you intend to serve."],
    ["Sampling and bias","A survey result depends on who could be contacted, who responded, and the question wording. Interviewing only enthusiastic users can miss people who abandoned the service. Recruit recent successes, failures, and non-users when the decision concerns adoption. Report the sample and limits instead of turning a small pattern into a population fact."],
    ["Market and competitor lenses","TAM, SAM, and SOM narrow a market from possible demand to serviceable demand and plausible share. Five Forces examines competitive pressure from rivals, entrants, substitutes, buyers, and suppliers. Neither calculation tells you why a person switches today. Pair market analysis with the actual customer journey."],
    ["Discoverability, use, retention","Someone may never find the product, may try it once, or may return regularly. These are different problems. Diagnose the stage before choosing marketing, onboarding, reliability, or habit changes. A growing sign-up count can coexist with weak first-use success and declining retention."]
  ],
  4:[
    ["Diverge, then converge","When evidence is incomplete, generate several plausible causes and solution directions. Then narrow using user evidence, feasibility, business fit, and risk. The Double Diamond makes these two expansions and contractions visible. It is a thinking pattern, not a promise that the process will be linear."],
    ["Five Whys and How Might We","Five Whys helps pursue a possible causal chain; stop when evidence ends, because a complex system can have more than one cause. How Might We turns an evidenced problem into a creative prompt. Keep it open enough for multiple options: ‘How might we make substitutions predictable?’ rather than ‘How might we add a bot?’"],
    ["MoSCoW, Kano, and RICE","MoSCoW clarifies what is required within a release scope. Kano explores which attributes are basic expectations, performance drivers, or delights. RICE compares estimated reach, impact, confidence, and effort. Use the tool that fits the question, and always show assumptions and mandatory constraints alongside the score."],
    ["Story, backlog, roadmap","A user story gives a delivery conversation a user goal; acceptance criteria make correct behavior observable. A backlog sequences concrete work. A roadmap communicates outcome bets and confidence over time. Mixing these levels produces a long feature list with no visible strategic choice."]
  ],
  5:[
    ["Information architecture and UX writing","Information architecture groups and names things so people can predict where to go. UX writing makes an action and its consequence clear at the moment of choice. In the grocery case, ‘Approve replacement’ is clearer than a generic ‘Continue’ when the shopper must accept a price change."],
    ["Accessibility is part of the task","Check that important information can be perceived, controls can be reached with a keyboard, focus is visible, text is readable, and errors can be recovered from. A flow that works only for a precise pointer or a wide screen has a narrower usable audience than its analytics may suggest."],
    ["Design systems and UX laws","A design system reuses components and interaction rules. UX laws and heuristics suggest where effort or confusion may occur. Neither guarantees a good experience: a reused component can still have the wrong label or behavior. Observe a real task and test whether the pattern helps."],
    ["PM, design, engineering together","The PM clarifies the job and outcome; design explores comprehension and flow; engineering tests feasibility, reliability, and cost. These are overlapping conversations, not a handoff chain. Bring operations in when the experience depends on a real-world service or recovery process."]
  ],
  6:[
    ["Choose the right artifact","Use a short brief to align on problem and decision, a roadmap to communicate direction, a PRD to bound a change, a prototype to make an interaction inspectable, and a project hub to keep the current decision and links findable. A business requirements document may capture organizational constraints; its name matters less than the decision it supports."],
    ["Progress and change updates","A useful progress update says what outcome is on track, what changed, what is blocked, who owns the next action, and when to expect another update. When a requirement changes, show scope, cost, timing, and user impact before silently replacing the earlier agreement."],
    ["Launch and incident messages","A launch note states audience, rollout, expected behavior, support path, success measure, and rollback condition. An incident note states confirmed impact, current mitigation, owner, and next update. Keep suspected cause separate until it is verified."],
    ["Tell an evidence-based story","STAR organizes situation, task, action, and result. Say what the team did, what you personally decided, and what was actually measured. If the result is unknown, say so and explain what was learned. A confident invented number makes a story weaker, not stronger."]
  ],
  7:[
    ["AARRR and HEART","AARRR groups growth questions into acquisition, activation, retention, referral, and revenue. HEART groups experience questions around happiness, engagement, adoption, retention, and task success. Choose dimensions relevant to the decision; a framework is not a requirement to track everything."],
    ["Instrument before analyzing","Define an event name, when it fires, properties, identity, exclusions, and the time window. Test edge cases such as retries, refunds, anonymous-to-signed-in changes, and delayed fulfilment. A funnel built on inconsistent events may show a reporting defect rather than a behavior change."],
    ["Cohorts and retention","Choose a starting cohort, a meaningful return action, and a period matching natural use. A weekly grocery shopper may be healthy without ordering daily. Compare cohorts with similar opportunity to return, and distinguish reactivation from continuous retention."],
    ["Marketplace and unit economics","A marketplace must create value for demand and supply. Track order contribution margin, acquisition cost, repeat use, refunds, and supply reliability separately. Revenue is not profit; gross merchandise value is not retained value. Improving a conversion rate can still damage the business if fulfilment cost rises faster."],
    ["Insight, funnel, flow, retention","An insight chart shows how a measure changes, a funnel locates progression loss, a flow chart reveals paths, and a retention view follows cohorts. Each report answers a different question. Write the decision first, then choose the chart and check data quality before interpreting it."]
  ]
};

